It publishes an **independent statistical audit** of the Ballot Box Reports (BU) of Brazil's 2026 General Elections, made with TSE public data.

- 499,207 sections analyzed in the 2nd round and 499,206 in the 1st round;
- 15 statistical and consistency tests;
- full reports in Portuguese, English and Spanish;
- source code and data for download, with SHA-512.

It is not an official TSE website.

The `dados/` folder of each round:

- `resumo_testes.csv` – the 15 tests: units, flags, informational events;
- `secoes_sinalizadas.csv` – every occurrence (test, section, machine, observed, reference, p, q);
- `busca_urnas.json` – one entry per section with every test it appears in;
- `secoes_convergentes.csv` – sections in two or more test families;
- `sinalizacoes_por_uf.csv` – sections and flagged sections by state;
- `unanimidade_por_candidato.csv` – 100% sections by candidate;
- `digitos_segundo_mebane.csv` and `digitos_ultimo_beber_scacco.csv` – tests 13 and 14;
- `klimek_indicadores.csv` – test 15;
- `classificacao_partidaria.csv` – classification for test 6;
- `integridade_arquivos.csv` – SHA-512 of the input files;
- `nucleo_analitico.json` – core for the determinism check.

Outside it: PT/EN/ES reports, `graficos/`, `log_execucao.txt` and `manifesto_saida.sha512`.

A flag is a **request to check**. The path is documentary:

1. Compare with the **printed BU** posted at the section (machine number, load code, votes);
2. Read the **polling station minutes** (machine replacement, incidents);
3. For technical analysis, the machine's signed files (.bu, .imgbu, .rdv, .logjez) are published by the TSE.

Also read the test's limitations.

Opening and closing times in the BU are in **local time**. Voting runs from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. **Brasília time**:

- Acre: 6 a.m. to 3 p.m. local;
- Amazonas (most of it), Rondônia, Roraima, Mato Grosso, Mato Grosso do Sul: 7 a.m. to 4 p.m. local;
- Abroad: 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. in the country's time.

Test 11 converts each municipality to the right time zone before comparing.

- `--dados` – folder with the TSE files (default `./dados_tse`);
- `--saida` – results folder (default `./resultados`);
- `--offline` – no internet access (no official hash and no CESP download);
- `--manter-banco` – keeps the intermediate DuckDB database in `<saida>/_trabalho`;
- `--manter-extraidos` – keeps the unzipped CSVs in `<saida>/_trabalho/csv`;
- `--baseline` – folder of the determinism baseline record (default `<saida>/.determinismo`).

In the `--dados` folder:

- `bweb_<round>t_<UF>_<date>.zip` – **preferred**: allows checking the official hash;
- `bweb_<round>t_<UF>_<date>.csv` – accepted, but the TSE official hash refers to the .zip;
- `CESP_<round>t_<UF>_<date>.zip` – optional; downloaded automatically if online.

2nd-round example: `bweb_2t_PR_261020260742.zip`.

Execution order (`Auditoria.executar`):

1. prepares folders;
2. finds the bweb and CESP files;
3. downloads missing CESP files;
4. computes SHA-512 and checks against the TSE;
5. reads the CSVs with DuckDB;
6. builds per-section bases and assigns reference groups;
7. runs the 15 tests;
8. consolidates (by state, convergent);
9. exports CSV/JSON and charts;
10. checks determinism;
11. generates the PT/EN/ES reports;
12. writes the output manifest.

The code has 3,193 lines, version 2.1.0.

I can answer about:

- **each test** ("what is test 5", "how is test 3 calculated", "result of test 12");
- **your section** ("PR zone 177 section 45");
- **results** by state or candidate ("result in Bahia");
- **the engine** (install, run, errors, parameters, functions);
- **the reports** (sections, columns, glossary);
- **downloads and SHA-512**.
